6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: 
The board after the given stones is

* Black C1–C2 : L = {B1,B2}
* Black A2 : L = {A1,A3,B2}
* Black B3–B4 : L = {A3,B2,A4}
* White D1–D2 : L = {E1,D3,E2} so it is not in atari. D3 is not a forced self-save.
* White C3–C4 : L = {D3,D4,C5}

The C1–C2 two-stone chain is the focal point. Its two liberties are B1 and B2. 
W B1 at once removes B1 from the chain. After W B1

C1–C2 L = {B2},  B1 L = {A1,B2}.

B2 is the only point that can give C1–C2 a new liberty.  A Black B2 after W B1 would be placed with neighbours A2 black, B1 white, B3 black, C2 black – four occupied neighbours and no capture of B1, which still keeps A1.  B2 is therefore illegal suicide and Black cannot answer on B2.  No other Black move creates a new liberty for C1–C2.  Hence the chain is dead and W B2 next move captures C1–C2 with a legal capture because the capture gives B2 the new liberty C2.

That makes W B1 a forced kill.  The claim that W B1 is atari on B2 and is captured by B2 is wrong: B1 keeps the A1 liberty after a B2 play, so B1 is not captured.

W B2 first is not forced.  After W B2 the white stone is in atari on B1 only.  Black B1 captures W B2 and restores a liberty to C1–C2 via the now empty B2.  After White B2 Black B1 the three black groups become

X = B1–C1–C2 L={A1,B2}
Y = A2 L={A1,A3,B2}
Z = B3–B4 L={A3,A4,B2}

and White has no immediate forced net; B2 and A1 are suicide after the capture, and the position is not a guaranteed capture.

Moves such as A1, A3, A4, D3, C5 etc. either are in atari themselves, are suicidal, or do not kill the black complex against best defence.  The D3 “save D1–D2” argument is based on a mis-count of D1–D2 liberties.

Current best assessment: the unique first move that starts a guaranteed kill sequence is B1.  Colleagues 11,2 point to the correct B1 net; colleagues 1,13,14,5,7,8 who put B2 first and colleague 4’s D3 save are mistaken; colleagues 3,12,4 who claim no winning first move underestimate the illegality of Black B2 after W B1.  No other first move is winning.

